<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Ballad · Lament · 1611 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Three Ravens</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old English ballad, printed in 1611, in which three ravens find a fallen knight still guarded by his loyal hawks and hounds.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>printed 1611; possibly older</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Ballad · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous; printed in Thomas Ravenscroft's Melismata (1611))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-three-ravens">The Three Ravens</a>&quot; (Roud 5, Child 26) is an English folk ballad printed in Melismata, a songbook compiled by Thomas Ravenscroft and published in 1611; the song may be older. Three scavenger birds talk over where and what they shall eat. One tells the others of a knight newly slain, but they find him guarded by his loyal hawks and hounds. Then a &quot;fallow doe&quot;, a figure for the knight's pregnant lover or mistress, his &quot;leman&quot;, comes to the body, kisses his wounds, carries him away and buries him, leaving the ravens without their meal, and the ballad closes by wishing every gentleman such hawks, such hounds and such a lover.</p><p>The 1611 text can be sung straight through in stanzas of four lines, or in stanzas of two lines with the first line repeated three times, which seems to be the more usual way; the refrains of nonsense syllables are sung in every stanza. Newer versions, with different music, were taken down up to the 19th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-james-child">Francis James Child</a> printed several of them as number 26 of his Child Ballads.</p><p>&quot;The Twa Corbies&quot;, in the Scots language, was clearly derived from it, though nobody knows how early it was first sung. Child quotes a letter of 8 August 1802 from Charles Kirkpatrick Sharpe to Walter Scott, saying that Sharpe had it from Miss Erskine of Alva, who had written it down from the recitation of an old woman at Alva; so it was already known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> by then. Scott first published it in his Minstrelsy of the Scottish Border in 1803, and William Motherwell printed it in Minstrelsy Ancient and Modern in 1827. Its tone is darker and more cynical. There are only two birds; the knight's hawk and hound have forsaken him for other game, his lady has already taken another lover, and nobody else knows where he lies or even that he is dead, so the birds can plan their meal in gruesome detail. It speaks of the fragility of life, of life going on after a death, and of loneliness and despair. It was one of 25 traditional works in Ballads Weird and Wonderful (1912), illustrated by Vernon Hill.</p><p>Both ballads have been translated into many languages, most often to the tune of &quot;The Twa Corbies&quot; or of the Breton song &quot;An Alarc'h&quot; (&quot;The Swan&quot;). In 1828 Aleksandr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Pushkin</a> published a partial Russian version of &quot;The Twa Corbies&quot;, made from a French translation of Scott's ballads; his &quot;Scottish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;, which ends on the lady waiting for a lover who is alive, became very well known among Russian speakers and was set by many composers of the time. The Danish folklorist Svend Grundtvig and Theodor Fontane translated the ballads in the 19th century, Nathan Alterman and Yaakov Shabtai into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, and the Basque poet Jon Mirande into Basque, sung by Imanol Larzabal. Folk and rock bands across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> have adapted them since, from Steeleye Span's &quot;Corbies&quot; to the German band Subway to Sally, who moved the story to the present and made the knight a soldier.</p><p>The ballad turns up in later writing and film. Lady Mevrian sings it in mourning for her brother in E. R. Eddison's fantasy novel The Worm Ouroboros (1922), and Dorothy L. Sayers quotes its last two lines in her mystery novel Clouds of Witness. A version by John Harle is heard in Simon Schama's A History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the song was chosen for the film My Cousin Rachel (2017), where it is sung at a Christmas feast, and for Firebrand (2023), where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> sings it.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A ballad of faithfulness beyond death. Three ravens looking for a meal find a slain knight, but his hounds lie at his feet and his hawks keep the birds away, and a doe heavy with young, the knight's lover, lifts his head, kisses his wounds, carries him off to bury him and dies herself before evening. The ballad ends by wishing every gentleman such faithful companions and such a love. The mood is grave and tender, set off by a lilting refrain of nonsense syllables.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There were three ravens sat on a tree,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Downe a downe, hay down, hay downe</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There were three ravens sat on a tree,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a downe</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There were three ravens sat on a tree,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They were as black as they might be.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a downe derrie, derrie, derrie, downe, downe</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The one of them said to his mate,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>‘Where shall we our breakfast take?’</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>‘Downe in yonder green field,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There lies a knight slain under his shield.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>‘His hounds they lie downe at his feet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>So well they can their master keep.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>‘His hawks they fly so eagerly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>There’s no fowle dare him come nie.’</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Downe there comes a fallow doe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>As great with yong as she might goe.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She lift up his bloudy hed,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And kist his wounds that were so red.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She got him up upon her backe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And carried him to earthen lake.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She buried him before the prime,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>She was dead herself ere even-song time.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God send every gentleman,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Such hawks, such hounds, and such a leman.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Thomas Ravenscroft, Melismata (1611), as printed by Francis James Child, The English and Scottish Popular Ballads (1882). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_English_and_Scottish_Popular_Ballads%2FPart_1%2FChapter_26" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
