<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Twa Corbies</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1803</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Scots ballad derived from it, first published by Walter Scott</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Scottish Song (Aleksandr Pushkin)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1828</span><span class="sg-chain-h">partial Russian translation of The Twa Corbies</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ravnene (Svend Grundtvig)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Danish translation of The Twa Corbies</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die drei Raben (Theodor Fontane)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die zwei Raben (Theodor Fontane)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German translation of The Twa Corbies</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bi beleak (Jon Mirande)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Basque translation of The Twa Corbies</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Válka růží (Spirituál kvintet)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the melody with new Czech words on the Wars of the Roses</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Krähenfraß (Subway to Sally)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">German adaptation of The Twa Corbies</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Worm Ouroboros (E. R. Eddison)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1922</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in a novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Clouds of Witness (Dorothy L. Sayers)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">last two lines quoted in a novel</span></li><li><a href="/songs/when-johnny-comes-marching-home">When Johnny Comes Marching Home</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1863</span><span class="sg-chain-h">possible connection (Jonathan Lighter)</span></li></ul></section>
